WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.720
In cloud environments,

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:06.740
identity is often the main control plane boundary. A network boundary still matters,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.600
but cloud APIs can create resources, change policies, attach storage,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:17.340
modify network rules, rotate keys, deploy workloads, and delete systems.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.680
If an identity can call those APIs with broad permissions,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.220
that identity can create broad risk.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.520
This is why identity and access management is central to cloud security.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:33.020
Cloud identity includes more than human users. It includes groups, roles,

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:38.280
service accounts, workload identities, API keys, temporary credentials,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.820
managed identities, and automation principals used by deployment pipelines.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.000
A developer might sign in with multi-factor authentication and assume a role for a limited

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.100
task.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.680
A container might use a workload identity to read a specific secret.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:58.100
A pipeline might use a service account to deploy approved resources. Each identity should

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.760
have a clear purpose,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.380
a defined owner, and permissions that match the work it actually needs to perform.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.800
Least privilege is the practical design goal. It means an identity can do the job it needs

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:10.580
to do,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.280
but not every adjacent job in the account, subscription, project, or tenant.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.380
Broad administrator access may be necessary for a small set of break-glass or platform

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:21.480
roles,

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.500
but it should be rare, monitored, and reviewed.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.400
Daily engineering work should use narrower roles. Read-only access, deployment access,

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.900
data access,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.680
and security administration should not be casually combined unless there is a documented

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:37.620
reason.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.960
Access should also be time-aware. Temporary credentials are safer than long-lived keys

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.500
because they reduce the lifetime of exposure.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.280
Privileged access should be justified and recorded. Dormant users,

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.300
unused service accounts, old API keys,

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.120
and abandoned workload identities should be reviewed and removed.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.580
Secrets should not be embedded in source code, images, tickets, chat messages,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.320
or local scripts.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.700
They should be stored in approved secret-management systems with access logging and

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.360
rotation processes.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.860
Separation of duties helps prevent accidental concentration of power. The person who

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.240
writes an application does not always need permission to approve production access

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:20.340
changes.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.680
A workload that reads from one storage bucket does not need write access to every bucket.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.140
A monitoring tool may need to read logs without being able to disable logging.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:36.280
These distinctions are engineering controls, not paperwork. A healthy IAM routine asks:

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:41.940
who or what has access, why, for how long, and how would we notice misuse or drift?

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.400
Permissions should be reviewed as systems change, not only during initial setup.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.980
Cloud security improves when identity is treated as live infrastructure: designed,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.800
documented, monitored, and continuously cleaned up.
